To solve the inequality |3x - 4| < 7, we consider two cases: when 3x - 4 is positive and when it's negative.

Case 1: 3x - 4 ≥ 0
3x ≥ 4
x ≥ &frac{4}{3}

Case 2: 3x - 4 < 0
3x < 4
x < &frac{4}{3}

Now, solve for each case:

For Case 1: |3x - 4| < 7 becomes 3x - 4 < 7
3x < 11
x < &frac{11}{3}
Combining with x ≥ &frac{4}{3}, we get &frac{4}{3} ≤ x < &frac{11}{3}

For Case 2: |3x - 4| < 7 becomes -(3x - 4) < 7
-3x + 4 < 7
-3x < 3
x > -&frac{3}{3}
Combining with x < &frac{4}{3}, we get -&frac{3}{3} < x < &frac{4}{3}

The combined solution is: -&frac{1}{3} < x < &frac{11}{3}

To solve the inequality |3x + 1| ≥ 9, we consider two cases: when 3x + 1 is non-negative and when it's negative.

Case 1: 3x + 1 ≤ 0
3x ≤ -1
x ≤ -&frac{1}{3}

Case 2: 3x + 1 > 0
3x > -1
x > -&frac{1}{3}

Now, solve for each case:

For Case 1: |3x + 1| ≥ 9 becomes 3x + 1 ≥ 9
3x ≥ 8
x ≥ &frac{8}{3}
Since x must also be less than or equal to -&frac{1}{3}, the solution for this case is x = -&frac{1}{3} (only one value)

For Case 2: |3x + 1| ≥ 9 becomes -(3x + 1) ≥ 9
-3x - 1 ≥ 9
-3x ≥ 10
x ≤ -&frac{10}{3}
Since x must also be greater than -&frac{1}{3}, the solution for this case is -&frac{1}{3} < x ≤ -&frac{10}{3}

The combined solution is: x = -&frac{1}{3} or -&frac{1}{3} < x ≤ -&frac{10}{3}
